1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,786
Start in the PAM project folder. Run docker ps to list the running containers.

2
00:00:04,786 --> 00:00:09,571
The Teleport container is running. Reset the existing admin account with tctl.

3
00:00:09,571 --> 00:00:14,643
Copy the one-time reset link and open it in the local browser.

4
00:00:14,643 --> 00:00:17,143
Select Continue to begin account recovery.

5
00:00:17,143 --> 00:00:25,143
Set and confirm a new password for the Teleport user admin.

6
00:00:25,143 --> 00:00:29,000
Register the authenticator and enter its verification code.

7
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:32,571
Open the cluster. The Resources page lists localhost as an SSH server.

8
00:00:32,571 --> 00:00:39,714
The sidebar provides access to identity settings, resources, and audit tools.

9
00:00:39,714 --> 00:00:43,214
On the localhost resource, open Connect and select the root login.

10
00:00:43,214 --> 00:00:46,714
In the browser terminal, pwd shows the working directory: /root.

11
00:00:46,714 --> 00:00:52,857
The echo command prints a test message inside the SSH session.

12
00:00:52,857 --> 00:01:01,286
A relative path fails here. Use the absolute path /root to return to the home directory.

13
00:01:01,286 --> 00:01:07,500
Return to the cluster and open Users to inspect local identities.

14
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:12,857
Select a user to inspect its assigned roles and traits.

15
00:01:12,857 --> 00:01:18,929
The interface also offers integrations and access-request workflows.

16
00:01:18,929 --> 00:01:25,357
Open the bot details to inspect the settings for a machine identity.

17
00:01:25,357 --> 00:01:31,786
In Instance Inventory, localhost appears with its Teleport version and services.

18
00:01:31,786 --> 00:01:37,714
Open Roles under Zero Trust Access to inspect the available policies.

19
00:01:37,714 --> 00:01:45,857
The role editor displays the policy definition and available editing options.

20
00:01:45,857 --> 00:01:54,286
Browse the cluster settings and open the integrations list.

21
00:01:54,286 --> 00:02:03,357
Machine ID shows bots and their instances in a separate inventory.

22
00:02:03,357 --> 00:02:09,286
Open the GitHub Actions integration to explore its Kubernetes setup guide.

23
00:02:09,286 --> 00:02:17,714
The wizard asks for repository details and shows a sample workflow.

24
00:02:17,714 --> 00:02:22,857
Open Audit, then Session Recordings, and select the recorded SSH session.

25
00:02:22,857 --> 00:02:30,357
The player identifies admin and localhost. Its timeline replays the terminal activity.

26
00:02:30,357 --> 00:02:36,429
Return to the session list, then open the resource enrollment catalog.

27
00:02:36,429 --> 00:02:41,286
The web application guide offers agent setup commands with a temporary join token.

28
00:02:41,286 --> 00:02:45,929
The Kubernetes guide shows the chart repository and cluster configuration fields.

29
00:02:45,929 --> 00:02:52,071
The Debian guide offers SSH service labels. Leave the wizard to open Audit.

30
00:02:52,071 --> 00:02:57,643
Select the join-token event to inspect its audit record.

31
00:02:57,643 --> 00:03:02,143
The log also records session access, disconnects, and authentication changes.

32
00:03:02,143 --> 00:03:09,429
Open Create User and enter a name for a local account.

33
00:03:09,429 --> 00:03:16,286
The form provides role selection and optional user traits.

34
00:03:16,286 --> 00:03:18,619
Return to the Users list.
